DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccaapp__ssyyssccttll(), ccaapp__ssyyssccttllbbyynnaammee() and ccaapp__ssyyssccttllnnaammeettoommiibb() functions
     are equivalent to sysctl(3), sysctlbyname(3) and sysctlnametomib(3), ex‐
     cept that they are implemented by the ‘system.sysctl’ libcasper(3) ser‐
     vice and require a corresponding libcasper(3) capability.

     All of these functions, with the exceptions of ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__iinniitt()
     and ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__mmiibb(), are reentrant but not thread-safe.  That is,
     they may be called from separate threads only with different
     _c_a_p___c_h_a_n_n_e_l___t arguments or with synchronization.

LLIIMMIITTSS
     By default, the ccaapp__ssyyssccttll capability provides unrestricted access to the
     sysctl namespace.  Applications typically only require access to a small
     number of sysctl variables; the ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt() interface can be used
     to restrict the sysctls that can be accessed using the ccaapp__ssyyssccttll capa‐
     bility.  ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__iinniitt() returns an opaque limit handle used to
     store a list of permitted sysctls and access rights.  Rights are encoded
     using the following flags:

           CAP_SYSCTL_READ         allow reads of the sysctl variable
           CAP_SYSCTL_WRITE        allow writes of the sysctl variable
           CAP_SYSCTL_RDWR         allow reads and writes of the sysctl variable
           CAP_RECURSIVE           permit access to any child of the sysctl variable

     The ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__nnaammee() function adds the sysctl identified by _n_a_m_e
     to the limit list, and ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__mmiibb() function adds the sysctl
     identified by _m_i_b_p to the limit list.  The access rights for the sysctl
     are specified in the _f_l_a_g_s parameter; at least one of CAP_SYSCTL_READ,
     CAP_SYSCTL_WRITE and CAP_SYSCTL_RDWR must be specified.
     ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt() applies a set of sysctl limits to the capability,
     denying access to sysctl variables not belonging to the set.  It consumes
     the limit handle.  After either success or failure, the user must not ac‐
     cess the handle again.

     Once a set of limits is applied, subsequent calls to ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt()
     will fail unless the new set is a subset of the current set.

     ccaapp__ssyyssccttllnnaammeettoommiibb() will succeed so long as the named sysctl variable
     is present in the limit set, regardless of its access rights.  When a
     sysctl variable name is added to a limit set, its MIB identifier is auto‐
     matically added to the set.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example first opens a capability to casper, uses this capa‐
     bility to create the ssyysstteemm..ssyyssccttll casper service, and then uses the
     ccaapp__ssyyssccttll capability to get the value of kern.trap_enotcap.

     cap_channel_t *capcas, *capsysctl;
     const char *name = "kern.trap_enotcap";
     void *limit;
     size_t size;
     bool value;

     /* Open capability to Casper. */
     capcas = cap_init();
     if (capcas == NULL)
             err(1, "Unable to contact Casper");

     /* Enter capability mode sandbox. */
     if (cap_enter() < 0 && errno != ENOSYS)
             err(1, "Unable to enter capability mode");

     /* Use Casper capability to create capability to the system.sysctl service. */
     capsysctl = cap_service_open(capcas, "system.sysctl");
     if (capsysctl == NULL)
             err(1, "Unable to open system.sysctl service");

     /* Close Casper capability, we don't need it anymore. */
     cap_close(capcas);

     /* Create limit for one MIB with read access only. */
     limit = cap_sysctl_limit_init(capsysctl);
     (void)cap_sysctl_limit_name(limit, name, CAP_SYSCTL_READ);

     /* Limit system.sysctl. */
     if (cap_sysctl_limit(limit) < 0)
             err(1, "Unable to set limits");

     /* Fetch value. */
     size = sizeof(value);
     if (cap_sysctlbyname(capsysctl, name, &value, &size, NULL, 0) < 0)
             err(1, "Unable to get value of sysctl");

     printf("The value of %s is %d.\n", name, value);

     cap_close(capsysctl);

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__iinniitt() will return a new limit handle on success or NULL
     on failure, and set _e_r_r_n_o.  ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__mmiibb() and
     ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt__nnaammee() will return the modified limit handle on success
     or NULL on failure and set _e_r_r_n_o.  After failure, the caller must not ac‐
     cess the limit handle again.  ccaapp__ssyyssccttll__lliimmiitt() will return -1 on fail‐
     ure and set _e_r_r_n_o.  ccaapp__ssyyssccttll(), ccaapp__ssyyssccttllbbyynnaammee(), and
     ccaapp__ssyyssccttllnnaammeettoommiibb() have the same return values as their non-capabil‐
     ity-mode equivalents as documented in sysctl(3).

